  "query_preview": "..\n\nResidual moisture remains in place across southeastern Arizona\nwith satellite measurements of precipitable water in the 0.8-1\ninch range this afternoon. The brunt of yesterday's moisture as\nwell as the forcing mechanism via the subtropical jet have \nexited the area however, which has significantly reduced today's\nprecipitation chances. Current forecast chances for this \nafternoon/evening are mainly tied to the White Mountains as well\nas slight but non-zero chances from Santa Cruz through Cochise \ncounties and areas near the New Mexico border. There should be \nenough moisture/instability for localized gusty winds or a brief\nheavy rain but otherwise the overall threat level has been \ngreatly reduced from yesterday. Today's forecast high \ntemperatures sit near to just below normal. Breezy afternoon \nwinds can be expected, with 15-20 mph winds and gusts to 35 mph \nthrough the Gila River valley. \n\nFlow aloft becomes zonal this weekend with daily westerly\nbreezes and high temperatures right about at normal. A mid-\nupper level ridge will build over northern Mexico and the \ndesert southwest through next week, which will bring a warm-up \nto southeastern Arizona. Chances for at least 105 degree highs \nspread across desert locations increasing from Monday through \nthe work week. Current forecasts have widespread Moderate to \nlocally High HeatRisk by the middle of next week.",
